18. fetch

fetch() is used in JavaScript and React to communicate with an API. An API allows our application to request data from another server. For example, a website might request a list of products from an API instead of keeping all products directly inside the React code.

A simple example is:

fetch("https://example.com/products")

However, fetch() does not immediately give us the final data. It starts a request, and the response comes back later. This is why fetch() is commonly used with asynchronous JavaScript.

A common way to use it is with .then():

fetch("https://example.com/products")
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
  });

The first .then() receives the response from the server. response.json() converts the response into JavaScript data, usually an array or object. The second .then() receives that converted data.

async and await

async and await provide another way to work with asynchronous code. They often make API code easier to read.

An async function is a function that can use await.

async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("https://example.com/products");
  const data = await response.json();

  console.log(data);
}

Here, await fetch(...) waits for the response from the server before continuing.

Then:

const data = await response.json();

waits for the response to be converted into JavaScript data.

It is important to understand that await does not mean that the entire website freezes while waiting. It means that this asynchronous function waits for that particular operation to finish before continuing.

In React, you will often see this pattern inside a function:

async function getProducts() {
  const response = await fetch("https://example.com/products");
  const data = await response.json();

  setProducts(data);
}

Students do not need to learn every detail of asynchronous programming immediately. At this stage, the main idea is:

fetch → wait for response → convert response to JSON → use the data.

19. Loading, Error, and Empty States

When getting data from an API, the application needs to handle different situations.

For example, while waiting for the API:

Loading...

If something goes wrong:

Something went wrong.

If the API works but there is no data:

No products found.

These are called states.

In React, we can use state to keep track of them:

const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
const [error, setError] = useState(false);
const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);

For example:

if (loading) {
  return <p>Loading...</p>;
}

if (error) {
  return <p>Something went wrong.</p>;
}

if (products.length === 0) {
  return <p>No products found.</p>;
}

This makes the application more realistic because an API request does not always finish immediately or successfully.

20. GET

GET is used when we want to get data from an API.

For example:

const response = await fetch("https://example.com/products");
const data = await response.json();

console.log(data);

This is normally a GET request.

GET can be used for things such as:

Get all products
Get all users
Get one product
Get comments
Get songs

For example, an API might provide:

GET /products

which means:

"Give me the products."

21. POST

POST is used when we want to send new data to an API.

For example, suppose we have a form for adding a product.

const newProduct = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 50
};

We can send it to the API:

const response = await fetch("https://example.com/products", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(newProduct)
});

JSON.stringify() converts the JavaScript object into JSON text that can be sent to the server.

The basic idea is:

GET = receive data

POST = create/send new data

22. PUT / PATCH

PUT and PATCH are used when we want to update existing data.

For example, suppose a product currently looks like this:

{
  id: 1,
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 50
}

We want to change the price.

With PUT, we might send the updated object:

const updatedProduct = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 60
};

await fetch("https://example.com/products/1", {
  method: "PUT",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(updatedProduct)
});

PATCH is also used for updating data, often when we only want to change part of an object.

For example:

await fetch("https://example.com/products/1", {
  method: "PATCH",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    price: 60
  })
});

For this level, students mainly need to remember:

PUT/PATCH = update existing data

23. DELETE

DELETE is used when we want to remove data.

For example:

await fetch("https://example.com/products/1", {
  method: "DELETE"
});

This tells the API to delete the product with ID 1.

The basic CRUD idea is:

GET     → Read
POST    → Create
PUT     → Update
PATCH   → Update
DELETE  → Delete

This is an important concept because many React applications use these four operations.

24. Small API Project

After learning the individual API operations, the student can combine them into one small React project.

A good beginner project could be a Product Manager.

The application could display products such as:

Products

Keyboard     $50     Edit   Delete
Mouse        $20     Edit   Delete
Monitor      $150    Edit   Delete

[ Add Product ]

The project could use:

GET

Load the products when the page starts.

POST

Add a new product using a form.

PUT/PATCH

Edit an existing product.

DELETE

Remove a product.

The application should also have:

Loading...

while the API request is happening.

If the request fails:

Failed to load products.

If there are no products:

No products available.

A simplified flow would be:

React starts
     ↓
GET products
     ↓
Show Loading...
     ↓
API responds
     ↓
Store products in state
     ↓
Display products

When adding:

Fill in form
     ↓
POST
     ↓
API saves product
     ↓
Update/display products

When editing:

Click Edit
     ↓
Change product
     ↓
PUT/PATCH
     ↓
API updates product
     ↓
Display updated product

When deleting:

Click Delete
     ↓
DELETE
     ↓
API removes product
     ↓
Remove/update product in React
